  "text": "A mathematical description was created for a smooth flow that hugs a surface when the fluid’s resistance to motion grows with temperature and the ratio of momentum to heat diffusion is taken as one. By stretching the coordinate that runs perpendicular to the surface, the governing equation becomes almost the same as the familiar one for non‑compressible flow, except that the pressure term is multiplied by a factor that is larger than one right at the wall and approaches one far away from it. The paper works out several example cases, such as a flow that speeds up linearly along the surface and one that slows down linearly on a flat plate. When the free‑stream speed starts from zero, climbs to a peak, then drops, the analysis shows that stronger density effects lower the wall shear, make the layer thicker, and cause the flow to detach sooner than in the equivalent constant‑density situation with the same free‑stream profile and viscosity measured at the stagnation point."
